"[{\"id\": \"D01\", \"item\": \"PGP staffing\", \"quote\": \"employs 30 people\", \"source\": \"p.12\", \"unit\": \"persons\", \"value\": \"30\"}, {\"id\": \"D02\", \"item\": \"PGP built date and ownership transition\", \"quote\": \"built in 1998/1999 ... In March 2016, Enterprise purchased the remaining 60 percent interest ... assumed operatorship on June 1, 2016\", \"source\": \"p.12\", \"unit\": \"date\", \"value\": \"built 1998/1999; Enterprise operatorship June 1, 2016\"}, {\"id\": \"D03\", \"item\": \"PGP design capacity\", \"quote\": \"design capacity of 1.5 billion cubic feet per day of raw gas and up to 50,000 barrels per day (bpd) of NGLs\", \"source\": \"p.58\", \"unit\": \"Bcf/d; bpd NGL\", \"value\": \"1.5 Bcf/d raw gas; 50,000 bpd NGL\"}, {\"id\": \"D04\", \"item\": \"PGP throughput at time of incident\", \"quote\": \"throughput was approximately 500 million standard cubic feet per day of raw gas and 16,000-18,000 bpd of NGLs\", \"source\": \"p.58\", \"unit\": \"MMscf/d; bpd NGL\", \"value\": \"~500 MMscf/d raw gas; 16,000-18,000 bpd NGL\"}, {\"id\": \"D05\", \"item\": \"Feed source\", \"quote\": \"processes raw natural gas it receives via the Destin pipeline from offshore production platforms in the Gulf of Mexico\", \"source\": \"p.17\", \"unit\": \"text\", \"value\": \"Destin pipeline, offshore Gulf of Mexico\"}, {\"id\": \"D06\", \"item\": \"Products leaving plant\", \"quote\": \"(1) a liquid referred to as natural gas condensate; (2) lighter natural gas, consisting mainly of methane; and (3) heavier hydrocarbons generally known as natural gas liquids (NGLs)\", \"source\": \"p.17\", \"unit\": \"text\", \"value\": \"condensate + residue gas (methane) + NGLs (ethane, propane, butanes, natural gasoline)\"}, {\"id\": \"D07\", \"item\": \"NGL export route\", \"quote\": \"sent through the Tri-States pipeline ... NGL storage bullets at the PGP site allow operators some flexibility\", \"source\": \"p.18; p.57\", \"unit\": \"text\", \"value\": \"Tri-States pipeline; onsite NGL storage bullets (capacity limited)\"}, {\"id\": \"D08\", \"item\": \"Residue gas export route\", \"quote\": \"gas product stream is sent through the Destin pipeline\", \"source\": \"p.57\", \"unit\": \"text\", \"value\": \"residue gas via Destin (black) pipeline; NGLs via Tri-States (red) pipeline\"}, {\"id\": \"D09\", \"item\": \"Personnel on duty night of incident\", \"quote\": \"two personnel on duty at PGP\u2014a control board operator and an outside operator\u2014were stopping production of natural gas liquids due to pipeline problems downstream\", \"source\": \"p.12; p.13\", \"unit\": \"persons\", \"value\": \"2 (board + outside); non-routine curtailment/shut-in of NGL production on A-Train\"}, {\"id\": \"D10\", \"item\": \"Initial explosion time and warnings\", \"quote\": \"at 11:22 p.m., a sudden explosion and fire occurred. No abnormal alarms or other indicators warned the two PGP personnel\", \"source\": \"p.13\", \"unit\": \"time\", \"value\": \"11:22 p.m. June 27, 2016; no prior alarm\"}, {\"id\": \"D11\", \"item\": \"Fire progression duration\", \"quote\": \"intensified several times over approximately the next 35 minutes as additional piping and equipment failed\", \"source\": \"p.14\", \"unit\": \"minutes\", \"value\": \"~35\"}, {\"id\": \"D12\", \"item\": \"Extent of damage\", \"quote\": \"Equipment, piping, and vessels in the A-Train process area were extensively damaged ... After the incident, 12 different breaches were identified in the A-Train equipment, piping, and vessels\", \"source\": \"p.14; p.62\", \"unit\": \"count\", \"value\": \"12 breaches identified post-incident (task statement cites 13 pieces ruptured - see RFI-01)\"}, {\"id\": \"D13\", \"item\": \"BAHX construction - integrity feature\", \"quote\": \"Critical to the structural integrity of the core are the brazed fin connections to the cap/parting sheet ... fins and braze joints hold the layers together, acting like a truss on a bridge\", \"source\": \"p.15\", \"unit\": \"text\", \"value\": \"stacked layers: parting sheets, fins, side bars, cap sheets; fin-to-sheet braze = truss\"}, {\"id\": \"D14\", \"item\": \"Thermal fatigue mechanism description\", \"quote\": \"aluminum parts push into and pull apart from each other ... Over time, this pushing and pulling fatigues the metal and creates thermal fatigue cracks that can allow process fluids to leak\", \"source\": \"p.16\", \"unit\": \"text\", \"value\": \"differential thermal expansion -> cyclic stress -> fatigue cracks -> leak to atmosphere or between passes\"}, {\"id\": \"D15\", \"item\": \"Thermal shock mechanism description\", \"quote\": \"Damage from thermal stresses can also result from a single large event, a phenomenon called thermal shock\", \"source\": \"p.16\", \"unit\": \"text\", \"value\": \"single large delta-T event can cause damage distinct from cyclic fatigue\"}, {\"id\": \"D16\", \"item\": \"Turboexpander outlet temperature\", \"quote\": \"One stream passes through a turboexpander, where its temperature is reduced to about -90 degF\", \"source\": \"p.59\", \"unit\": \"degF\", \"value\": \"about -90\"}, {\"id\": \"D17\", \"item\": \"Cryogenic plant definition\", \"quote\": \"capable of producing natural gas liquid products, including ethane, at very low operating temperatures, usually below minus 50 degF\", \"source\": \"p.12 n.7\", \"unit\": \"degF\", \"value\": \"usually below -50\"}, {\"id\": \"D18\", \"item\": \"DCS evidence - ACSR metal sensors stop\", \"quote\": \"ACSR bottom metal temperature sensor stopped transmitting between 11:20 and 11:21 p.m., quickly followed by cessation of signal transmission from the remaining metal sensors during the next minute\", \"source\": \"p.61\", \"unit\": \"time\", \"value\": \"ACSR bottom metal T 11:20-11:21 p.m. stop; remainder within next minute\"}, {\"id\": \"D19\", \"item\": \"DCS evidence - differential pressure drop indicates core rupture\", \"quote\": \"differential pressure of both the A and B passes began dropping between 11:21 p.m. and 11:22 p.m., most likely indicating a rupture of the core that compromised the integrity of both passes simultaneously\", \"source\": \"p.61\", \"unit\": \"time\", \"value\": \"A- and B-pass dP drop 11:21-11:22 p.m.; ACSR dP sensor stopped transmitting by 11:24 p.m.\"}, {\"id\": \"D20\", \"item\": \"DCS evidence - AWSR response lagged ACSR\", \"quote\": \"differential pressures in the B pass of the other reboiler ... AWSR, showed steady differential pressure ... until sometime between 11:22 and 11:23 p.m., when the reading dropped slightly, and then the sensor stopped transmitting between 11:23 and 11:24 p.m. ... metal temperature sensors on the AWSR also stopped transmitting between 11:23 and 11:25 p.m.\", \"source\": \"p.61\", \"unit\": \"time\", \"value\": \"AWSR B-pass dP steady then drop 11:22-11:23, stop 11:23-11:24; metal T stop 11:23-11:25\"}, {\"id\": \"D21\", \"item\": \"DCS evidence - AWSR A-pass drop during curtailment\", \"quote\": \"A pass differential pressure on the AWSR began dropping at 10:50 p.m., when the operator began making process changes following the curtailment procedures\", \"source\": \"p.61 n.157\", \"unit\": \"time\", \"value\": \"10:50 p.m. start of curtailment-related A-pass dP drop on AWSR\"}, {\"id\": \"D22\", \"item\": \"Laboratory finding\", \"quote\": \"post-incident laboratory examination of the exchanger revealed clear evidence of service-related thermal fatigue at several locations ... Available evidence eliminated several other well-known potential damage mechanisms (Table 1)\", \"source\": \"p.22\", \"unit\": \"text\", \"value\": \"clear thermal fatigue; other mechanisms eliminated per Table 1\"}, {\"id\": \"D23\", \"item\": \"Thermal-cycling process driver - B-pass instability\", \"quote\": \"when operators ... reduce or completely stop production ... boiling regime inside the exchanger can also change, leading to a fluctuating pressure differential ... conditions within the B pass can become unstable; rapid, localized temperature fluctuations can occur. Portions of the B pass layers can oscillate between liquid (which transfers heat quickly) and vapor\", \"source\": \"p.34\", \"unit\": \"text\", \"value\": \"turndown/shutdown -> boiling-regime change -> B-pass flow/pressure instability -> liquid-vapor oscillation -> localized rapid T fluctuation\"}, {\"id\": \"D24\", \"item\": \"Historical ROC exceedance magnitude\", \"quote\": \"To illustrate the magnitude of the ROCs the ACSR saw over its history, Figure 28 displays ... 1.8F-3.6F/min, 3.6F-12.6F/min, 12.6F-21.6F/min, 21.6F-30.6F/min, and greater than 30.6F/min ... 1.8F-3.6F/min and 3.6F-12.6F/ ... ; Figure 29. ROC data from Jan 1, 2011 to June 27, 2016, normalized to A-Train run time\", \"source\": \"p.37; p.38\", \"unit\": \"degF/min\", \"value\": \"ACSR routinely saw ROCs well above 3.6 limit, including >30.6 bins; 2011-2016 record analyzed\"}, {\"id\": \"D25\", \"item\": \"Metal vs stream ROC relationship\", \"quote\": \"ROCs calculated from the metal temperatures are about 10 times less than those for the stream temperatures [Fig.29 caption] / are about four times less than those for the stream temperatures. For example, in 2011, ROC exceedances for the metal sensors occurred between 2 and 3 percent of the t ...\", \"source\": \"p.38\", \"unit\": \"ratio; % time\", \"value\": \"metal ROC ~4x to ~10x less than stream ROC (conflict - see RFI); metal exceedances 2-3% of time in 2011\"}, {\"id\": \"D26\", \"item\": \"ACSR repair count\", \"quote\": \"HETSCO repaired the ACSR four times, most recently in September 2015. A total of 16 defects were fixed ... five leaks on side bars (9,11,12,13,14), nine cracks on cap sheet (1,2,3,4,5,6,8,15,16), one leak on cap sheet under attachment plate (7), and two pass sealings on either end (10)\", \"source\": \"p.66\", \"unit\": \"count\", \"value\": \"4 repairs; 16 defects; last Sept 2015 (Sept 16, 2015 per Table 8)\"}, {\"id\": \"D27\", \"item\": \"ACSR repair chronology (LOC vs crack)\", \"quote\": \"Table 8. Repair history of the ACSR ... Documentation does not state which crack resulted in the leak that needed repair [for early repairs]; dates 10/16/2007, 9/15/2011, 10/23/2014, 9/16/2015\", \"source\": \"p.67\", \"unit\": \"date; text\", \"value\": \"Repairs 1-2: 10/16/2007 LOC; 3-7: 9/15/2011 LOC; 8-12: 10/23/2014 LOC; 13-16: 9/16/2015 LOC; plus cracks; exact LOC crack ambiguous\"}, {\"id\": \"D28\", \"item\": \"B-Train cold side reboiler repairs\", \"quote\": \"taken out of service for repair three times: (1 & 2) two cracks on cap sheet, and (3) sealing passes 97, 98, and 99 ... 4/12/2007 LOC, 2/26/2010 LOC, 3/7/2012 LOC\", \"source\": \"p.67\", \"unit\": \"date\", \"value\": \"3 repairs; 2 cap-sheet cracks + sealing passes 97-99\"}, {\"id\": \"D29\", \"item\": \"Warm side reboiler repairs\", \"quote\": \"AWSR has only one recorded repair. HETSCO welded all four edges along the joint where the cap sheet and attachment plate meet ... 1/8/2008 ... B-Train warm side ... repaired only once ... 9/21/2010\", \"source\": \"p.68\", \"unit\": \"date\", \"value\": \"AWSR 1/8/2008 cap-to-attachment-plate 4 welds; B-warm 9/21/2010 same crack\"}, {\"id\": \"D30\", \"item\": \"Repair method types\", \"quote\": \"three repair types: (1) internal leaks between two passes would be repaired by welding the leaking passes closed, (2) a leaking side bar would be ground out and then welded over, and (3) crack on cap sheet would be ground out and filled with weld material ... inspectors completed examination for additional leaks or cracking on external surfaces\", \"source\": \"p.66\", \"unit\": \"text\", \"value\": \"block/seal passes; grind-and-weld side bar; grind-and-fill cap sheet; external-only inspection\"}, {\"id\": \"D31\", \"item\": \"Blocked-pass vent practice\", \"quote\": \"BP asked if HETSCO's other clients had success reducing thermal stresses when passes were blocked and if Chart would approve ... answer to both was yes ... vent holes made during repairs were sealed ... Some HETSCO personnel indicated in recent years they left holes open but filled with silicone ... will break loose to relieve process fluids\", \"source\": \"p.44; p.45\", \"unit\": \"text\", \"value\": \"blocked passes with sealed vent holes at PGP; silicone-filled (ventable) practice elsewhere\"}, {\"id\": \"D32\", \"item\": \"Fire-service response\", \"quote\": \"first of several local fire departments was dispatched ... approximately four minutes after the initial explosion ... decided ... to allow the fire to burn itself out, while cooling equipment where feasible\", \"source\": \"p.14; p.20\", \"unit\": \"minutes\", \"value\": \"~4 min dispatch; unified command; let-burn + cooling\"}]}"